insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at,
  photo_url, photo_alt, photo_credit, photo_source)
values
  ('when-you-did-not-go-to-the-funeral', 'When You Did Not Go to the Funeral', 'You could not go, or chose not to, and now the grief has nowhere to have started. Why missing the service complicates things, and how to mark a death after the fact.', 'You were not there. The distance, the money, the dates, the job, the illness, the family you would have had to sit with, the fact that nobody told you until afterward. Or you could have gone and decided not to.

Either way the day passed somewhere else, and you have been carrying something since that does not quite behave like the grief other people seem to be having.

What a funeral actually does

It is easy to dismiss a service as a formality. It does a few specific things, which is why missing it leaves a gap.

It marks a beginning. Grief without a starting point can stay oddly unanchored, as though the death has not been formally entered into the record of your life.

It makes the death real in company. Being in a room where everyone knows is different from knowing alone, and that difference does a lot of work.

It gives grief somewhere to be. For a few hours it is the whole point of the day, rather than something you manage around work.

And it hands you a shared story. The things people said, the order of events, the small details everyone refers to afterward. Without that, you are grieving a version nobody else is holding.

Why the absence keeps mattering

Most people expect the regret to fade and find that it does not, because it is not only regret. It is an unfinished piece.

There is often guilt, which arrives regardless of whether the reason was good. People who genuinely could not travel still feel it, which tells you the feeling is not really a verdict on the decision.

There is the explaining, which keeps the wound open. Every time somebody mentions the service you are reminded, and a short answer rarely feels like enough.

And there is the exclusion, particularly where you were not told, or were told late, or were not welcome. That is a second loss sitting on top of the first, and it deserves to be counted separately.

Marking it afterward, on purpose

A funeral can be held late and privately, and it works better than people expect. The point is not to replicate the service. It is to give the death a day.

Choose a time and keep it. An evening, a Saturday morning, the anniversary. Something with a beginning and an end rather than a vague intention.

Do something physical. Go to a place that meant something. Light a candle. Cook what they cooked. Put a photograph somewhere it will stay. Grief responds to objects and places more than to thinking.

Say something out loud, even alone. People consistently report that the speaking is the part that mattered, and that it felt strange for about ten seconds.

Write them a letter, and keep it or do not. Write what you would have said at the service, and what you could not say.

Invite one person, if there is anyone. A sister on a video call, a friend who knew them, somebody who will sit with you for an hour. Shared is different from private, and if the shared part is what you missed, this is the piece that supplies it.

Getting the story you did not get

If you can, ask someone who was there to tell you about it. What was read, who spoke, what was said, what the weather did. People are usually glad to be asked and it is more settling than it sounds.

Ask for the order of service or the recording if there is one. Many are kept.

And if there is nobody to ask, write down what you know of the end yourself: when, where, who was there. A plain record gives the mind something to hold instead of a blank.

If the reason you did not go was complicated

Sometimes the honest answer is not that you could not. It is that the family would have been unbearable, or you were not wanted, or the relationship was bad enough that going would have been a performance.

Those are legitimate reasons, and the grief underneath them is often harder rather than simpler, because it sits alongside anger and relief.

It helps to separate the two questions. Whether going would have been right for you is one thing. Whether you are allowed to grieve is another, and the answer to the second is yes regardless of the first.

What to say when people ask

Short and without the case for the defense. I could not get there. I was not able to go. We were not in touch at the end.

Most people accept a plain sentence and move on. The ones who push are usually working through something of their own, and you are not obliged to supply the explanation.

When to talk it through with someone

If the fact of not being there has become the main shape of your grief, if months have passed without it easing, if you are avoiding people connected to them, or if you cannot get to the grief at all because the guilt is in front of it, that is worth taking to a therapist.

Missed funerals and complicated goodbyes are common ground in grief work, and the approach is practical: giving the loss a marker, separating guilt from grief, and finding the mourning that the day was supposed to begin. Your doctor can point you to local grief services, many of them free. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Grief', 'Loss']::text[], 0, '2026-11-18T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-did-not-go-to-the-funeral.jpg', 'A single candle burning in a dark room', 'Photo by George Becker via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/candle-light-IB1L0U71X9'),
  ('when-your-clothes-stopped-fitting', 'When Your Clothes Stopped Fitting', 'A closet full of things that do not fit turns getting dressed into a daily verdict. Why that hurts more than the size itself, and the practical moves that take the sting out of the morning.', 'You open the closet and most of what is in there is not an option. The things that fit are the same four items you have been wearing. Everything else is a reminder, and getting dressed has become a small daily test you keep failing.

Bodies change. Illness, medication, pregnancy, injury, age, a hard year, a good year. The change is ordinary. What makes it heavy is the closet, which is quietly keeping score.

Why it lands harder than the size

Because clothes are not neutral objects. A particular shirt is attached to a job you had, a trip you took, a version of you that was doing well.

So what you are meeting at eight in the morning is not a waistband. It is a comparison, and the comparison is happening before you are properly awake and in front of a mirror.

There is also the practical part that nobody warns people about. Clothes that do not fit are uncomfortable all day, and discomfort keeps your attention on your body for hours, which is where the self-consciousness lives.

And it tends to be secret. Few people say out loud that getting dressed has become upsetting, which makes it feel like a personal failing instead of something extremely common.

The closet is the problem, not you

The single most useful change is to stop keeping a daily reminder in the place you have to look every morning.

Take everything that does not fit out of sight. Not necessarily out of the house, if that feels like a decision you are not ready to make. A box, a bag, the top of the closet, a different room. Out of the thing you open each day.

Keep only what fits, and make it visible. A smaller closet of workable clothes is better than a large one that is mostly a museum.

Give yourself a date rather than a verdict. In six months I will look at the box again is a sentence that lets you stop deciding today. Most people find that when the date arrives they are much less attached.

And if the clothes are genuinely finished, letting them go is a relief rather than a defeat. People report it as lighter than expected, which tells you what they were costing.

Buying a few things that actually fit

This is the part people resist, because it feels like accepting something. It is the part that changes the morning.

Buy for the body you have today, not the one you are hoping for. Clothes bought as motivation do not motivate. They sit there and accuse.

Prioritize comfort you can forget about. The test is whether you stop noticing it by mid-morning. Anything you adjust all day keeps your attention where it hurts.

You need fewer items than you think. Two or three things that fit well, that you can put on without thinking, will carry most of a week and most of the difficulty.

And ignore the number on the label. Sizes are not consistent between stores and never have been, so the label is not information about you. Cut it out if it helps, which is a thing plenty of people do.

The morning itself

Decide the night before when you can. The decision is harder in the moment than it looks the evening before.

Have a default outfit that always works, and allow yourself to repeat it. Nobody is keeping the record you think they are.

Be careful with mirrors at the exact moment of dressing. Checking once, dressed, is different from standing in front of one while changing, which is where most of the damage happens.

And notice what you say to yourself while you do it. Most people would never speak to another person that way about their body, and the running commentary is doing more harm than the fit of anything.

What this is not

It is not a reason to wait for your life to resume. People put off seeing friends, swimming, photographs and dates because of a closet, and the waiting costs far more than the clothes ever did.

And it is not a measure of how you are doing. Bodies change for dozens of reasons, many of them nothing to do with anything you chose, and plenty of them signs that something was treated.

When to bring someone in

See a doctor if your body changed quickly or without explanation, or alongside a new medication, since that is worth understanding rather than absorbing.

If getting dressed has started to govern your day, if you are avoiding people or canceling plans over it, if you are spending a large share of your attention on your body, or if the rules about what you are allowed to eat or wear keep tightening, that is worth taking to a therapist. This is common ground and very workable, and it is usually about the commentary rather than the clothes.', array['Shame', 'Body']::text[], 0, '2026-11-18T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-your-clothes-stopped-fitting.jpg', 'Shirts hanging on a line in front of a window', 'Photo by Jay Mantri via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/clothes-shirts-BD2F94946C'),
  ('finding-out-someone-earns-more-than-you', 'Finding Out Someone Earns More Than You', 'You learn what a colleague makes and the number will not leave you alone. How to separate the unfairness question from the sting, and what to actually do with the information.', 'Somebody left a document open, or said it outright, or you worked it out. Now you know what they earn, and it is more than you, and the number has moved into your head and will not leave.

The information is useful. The way it arrives is not, because it usually turns up without any of the context that would make sense of it, and in the meantime it is doing something to how you feel about your work.

Why it stings out of proportion

Pay is treated as a score. Nobody says so, but it operates as a summary of how much you are valued, and finding out somebody scored higher reads as a judgment rather than a line in a budget.

It also arrives retroactively. The gap was probably there for a year or two, and now every month you worked happily feels differently in hindsight.

And it is usually incomplete. You rarely learn the whole picture at once, which leaves the mind to fill in the rest, and the mind fills it in with the least flattering explanation available.

Then there is the colleague. You now know something about them that they did not choose to tell you, and that quietly changes an ordinary working relationship.

Separating two different questions

Almost everybody mixes these up, and sorting them out is most of the work.

The first is whether something unfair is happening. That is a factual question about the same role, the same level, the same work, and about whether the difference tracks something legitimate.

The second is the sting, which is about being valued, and which does not go away even when the answer to the first question turns out to be reasonable.

Both are real. They just need different responses, and treating the sting as proof of unfairness is what leads people into a conversation they have not prepared for.

What legitimately explains a gap

Worth knowing, because some of these are ordinary and some are not.

When each of you was hired. Pay offered at a particular time tends to stick, and someone hired in a hot market is often ahead of someone who has been loyal for years. This is extremely common and it is usually about the employer''s habits rather than anyone''s worth.

Whether they negotiated. A large share of gaps come down to one conversation at the start that one person had and the other did not.

Different responsibilities that are not visible from where you sit, or a different level on paper even though the day looks similar.

Prior experience, a competing offer they had, or a specialty that is scarce.

And none of that applies when the work really is the same and the difference tracks something it should not, which does happen and is a different conversation.

What to do with the information

Find out your market rate before anything else. Not what your colleague earns, which is one data point and may itself be odd, but what the role pays elsewhere. That is the number that gives you standing.

Write down what you have done this year, specifically, with outcomes. Most people arrive at a pay conversation able to describe their job and unable to describe their contribution, which is the thing that moves numbers.

Ask about your own pay, not theirs. I would like to talk about my salary, here is what the role pays elsewhere, here is what I have delivered. That conversation can succeed. One that opens with what someone else earns usually turns into a conversation about how you found out.

Do not reveal how you learned it if it would land on the colleague. They did not do anything to you, and their position is often more awkward than yours.

And ask what it would take. If the answer is not now, the useful follow-up is what would have to be true, and when, written down.

If nothing changes

Give it a date rather than an open-ended wait. Six months, reviewed.

Decide what you will do if the answer stays no, before you are upset. Stay and stop giving the extra, look elsewhere, ask again at a specific point. Any of those is a position. Resentment without a plan is the one option that costs you and changes nothing.

And protect the relationship with the colleague, who is not the cause. Carrying the number into every interaction with them is a real risk and it only costs you.

When it is more than a pay question

If this has become the thing you think about most, if it has you feeling worthless rather than underpaid, or if a difference you believe is discriminatory is being dismissed, those are worth taking further.

A therapist can help with the part that has attached your sense of worth to a number, which is more common than people admit. And if the gap looks like it tracks who you are rather than what you do, that is worth raising with someone qualified to advise you, rather than carried alone or settled by a professional guess.', array['Money', 'Work']::text[], 0, '2026-11-18T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/finding-out-someone-earns-more-than-you.jpg', 'Push pins and binder clips on a yellow background', 'Photo by Artsy Crafty via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/office-supplies-DZZLYOIPFB'),
  ('when-you-have-not-slept-properly-in-years', 'When You Have Not Slept Properly in Years', 'Long stretches of broken nights are a different problem from insomnia. What accumulates, which sleep is worth protecting first, and what helps when the interruptions are not optional.', 'Someone has been waking you for years. A baby, then a toddler, then a child with bad dreams, or a person you care for, or a job with a phone that goes off.

This is not insomnia. You can sleep perfectly well, given the chance, and you are not getting the chance, which means most sleep advice is written for somebody else''s problem.

What accumulates

Short-term broken sleep makes you tired. A long stretch of it does something broader, and it is worth naming so you stop reading it as a character change.

Your patience goes first. Small things take a disproportionate reaction, and that is a threshold effect rather than a decision.

Memory and words go next. Losing names, losing the thread mid-sentence, reading the same paragraph three times. Most people find this frightening and it is the ordinary consequence of years of interruption.

Mood drops and stays dropped. Long-term broken sleep lowers the floor, which is why a stretch like this can look and feel like something more, and why it is worth getting checked rather than assumed.

And the interruptions matter as much as the hours. Six hours in one piece is a very different thing from eight in forty-minute installments, because the sleep that does the restorative work needs an uninterrupted run at it.

Which sleep to protect first

If you cannot have all of it, there is an order worth knowing.

Protect one long unbroken stretch over total hours. If anyone else can take a shift, use it for a four or five hour block rather than two separate naps.

Protect the first half of the night when you can. The deepest sleep is loaded there, which is why going to bed at eleven and being woken at one is harder than it sounds.

Keep your wake time steadier than your bedtime. Steady waking is what keeps the whole rhythm from drifting, and it is the one thing you can usually control.

And take the nap, properly. Twenty minutes early in the afternoon, or a full ninety if you have it. Thirty to sixty is the window that leaves you worse, which is why people conclude naps do not work for them.

Sharing the nights, if there is anyone to share with

This is the single largest lever and it is usually underused, because the pattern sets itself in the first weeks and then nobody revisits it.

Split by block rather than by turn. One person covers until two, the other after, so each gets a real stretch. Alternating every waking means two broken nights instead of one.

Alternate whole nights where that is possible. Many people find one properly bad night and one good one much easier than two mediocre ones.

Be explicit that being the lighter sleeper is not a job assignment. It is extremely common for one person to end up doing all of it because they wake first, and that is worth naming rather than resenting.

And if you are doing it alone, the equivalent is asking somebody for one block, once a week. A relative, a friend, a few hours of paid help. One guaranteed long sleep changes a week more than people expect.

What helps around the edges

Lower the cost of the wakings themselves. Everything within reach, dim light, nothing bright, no phone. How quickly you get back down matters as much as how often you are up.

Go to bed earlier rather than trying to sleep longer at the other end. It is the only hour available to you, and the evening is where most people lose it to a screen.

Get outside in the morning light. It will not give you hours back and it does help the sleep you get be better placed.

Be careful with alcohol, which costs you the second half of the night, and with caffeine after about midday, which is hard advice for exactly the people who need it most.

And lower the bar on everything else, deliberately. Fewer commitments during a stretch like this is not indulgence, it is the only adjustment available.

When it is more than tiredness

If the interruptions have stopped and you still cannot sleep, that is a different problem and there is specific, effective treatment for it, usually short.

See a doctor if you snore heavily or wake gasping, if you are exhausted after a night that was not interrupted, or if you are falling asleep in the day in ways that are unsafe, since several treatable physical causes look exactly like this.

And raise it with a doctor or a therapist if your mood has been low for weeks, if you are frightened by how you react to the person waking you, or if you are getting through on a level of exhaustion that no longer feels survivable. Long-term broken sleep is a reason to get help, not a thing to simply absorb.', array['Sleep', 'Parenting']::text[], 0, '2026-11-18T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-have-not-slept-properly-in-years.jpg', 'A lit table lamp against patterned wallpaper', 'Photo via rawpixel, CC0', 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/6055148/free-public-domain-cc0-photo'),
  ('running-into-someone-you-used-to-know', 'Running Into Someone You Used to Know', 'You see them across the store and have about four seconds to decide. Why these encounters are so uncomfortable, what to actually say, and how to stop replaying it afterward.', 'You spot them before they spot you. A former colleague, someone from school, a friend you let drift, an ex''s sibling. Four seconds to decide whether to wave, say something, or develop a sudden interest in the shelf beside you.

Then whatever happens, you spend part of the afternoon going back over it.

Why four seconds is so hard

There is no script. Most social situations come with one, and this one does not: you do not know what you are to each other now, and you have to decide in the time it takes to walk past.

The information is also missing. You do not know whether they remember the last conversation the way you do, whether they know what happened to you since, whether they even recognize you.

And the gap has to be accounted for somehow. Any exchange carries an implicit question about why you have not spoken in three years, and both of you can feel it.

Then there is the mismatch. They have a vague warm memory and you have a specific one, or the reverse, and neither of you knows which.

What to actually say

Short, warm, and without an explanation for the silence. That combination handles almost all of these.

Lead with their name and something plain. It is good to see you. How long has it been. You look well.

Do not account for the gap. Nobody needs it, and launching into an explanation signals that the silence was loaded, which it usually was not for them.

Ask one specific question rather than how are you, which produces nothing. Are you still at the same place. Is your mother doing all right. How did the move go.

Then give it an ending yourself, which is the part people forget. I am glad I ran into you, I have to get this stuff home. Walking away cleanly is what stops these dragging into something neither of you wanted.

Three or four exchanges is a complete encounter. It does not have to go anywhere.

If you do not want to talk

That is allowed, and a small amount of acknowledgment is easier than hiding.

A nod and a smile while continuing to walk is a complete interaction and nobody reads it as rude.

If you genuinely cannot face it, going the other way is not a crime. It is worth knowing, though, that the guilt afterward is usually larger than the conversation would have been.

And if it is somebody you have reason to avoid, you are not obliged to be available because you are both in the same store. Leaving is a reasonable thing to do.

The replaying afterward

This is often the worst part, and it runs on a predictable script.

You will remember what you said and not what they said, which produces a very lopsided account of how it went.

You will grade the performance, usually on things nobody else registered: that you said good twice, that you did not ask about the job, that you walked away oddly.

And you will decide how it landed with no information at all. The certainty is the tell, as with most of this: with nothing to go on, confidence is coming from your mood rather than from evidence.

What helps is a time limit and a plain sentence. I saw someone, it was slightly awkward, it is finished. Then do something with your hands.

And it is worth knowing that the other person is almost certainly not doing this. Most people walk away from these with no more than a passing thought.

Deciding what to do next

If it went well and you want to see them, say so immediately rather than leaving it to a vague let us catch up. A specific suggestion within a day or two is the only version that happens.

If you do not want to, that is fine and requires nothing. A drifted friendship is not an unpaid debt.

And if it stirred something up, a loss, a job that ended badly, a version of yourself you do not want back, the encounter is not really the subject. Worth noticing, rather than analyzing the four seconds.

When it is more than awkward

If you are structuring your days around not running into people, going to different stores, avoiding a neighborhood, not answering the door, that has grown past discomfort into something that is shrinking your life.

That is well-trodden ground for a therapist, and the work on it is practical and usually short: what the fear is predicting, what actually happens, and how to get the two closer together. If a particular person is the reason you are avoiding public places, bring that to a professional too, because it is a different problem and needs a different answer.', array['Social Anxiety', 'Communication']::text[], 0, '2026-11-18T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/running-into-someone-you-used-to-know.jpg', 'An apartment building at dusk with a car parked outside', 'Photo by Matt Bango via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/car-street-U2NCFLUASF')
on conflict (slug) do update set
